development of Hatha Yoga."},{"sourceSlug":"Raja Yoga","sourceName":"Raja Yoga","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","targetName":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","context":"The Yoga Sutras of Patanjali have influenced the development of Raja Yoga."},{"sourceSlug":"Kundalini Yoga","sourceName":"Kundalini Yoga","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","targetName":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","context":"The Yoga Sutras of Patanjali have influenced the development of Kundalini Yoga."},{"sourceSlug":"India","sourceName":"India","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","targetName":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","context":"The Yoga Sutras of Patanjali were written in ancient India."},{"sourceSlug":"Bhavishya Purana","sourceName":"Bhavishya Purana","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","targetName":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","context":"The Bhavishya Purana contains references to the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali."}],"places":[{"name":"Ancient India","role":"Place of creation"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Yoga Sutras of Patanjali","type":"Reference article","slug":"yoga-sutras-of-patanjali"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1068646","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoga_Sutras_of_Patanjali"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Yoga Sutras of Patanjali contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Yoga Sūtras of Patañjali (Sanskrit: योगसूत्र, Yoga Sūtra) is a foundational text of classical Indian philosophy and the primary systematic exposition of Yoga as a philosophical system, attributed to the sage Patañjali and composed c. 400 CE (with scholarly estimates ranging from c. 200 BCE to c. 400 CE). The text consists of 196 sūtras (aphoristic formulas) divided into four pādas (chapters): Samādhi Pāda (51 sūtras, on the nature and purpose of yoga and its highest stages); Sādhana Pāda (55 sūtras, on the practice of yoga — the Eight Limbs, aṣṭāṅga yoga); Vibhūti Pāda (56 sūtras, on the supernatural powers — siddhis — that arise through practice); and Kaivalya Pāda (34 sūtras, on liberation, kaivalya, and the nature of consciousness).

The Yoga Sūtras define yoga as 'citta-vṛtti-nirodha' ('the cessation of the fluctuations of consciousness') and present the path to liberation through the Eight Limbs (Aṣṭāṅga Yoga): Yama (ethical restraints — non-violence, truthfulness, non-stealing, celibacy, non-possessiveness); Niyama (observances — purity, contentment, discipline, study, surrender to the divine); Āsana (posture); Prāṇāyāma (breath control); Pratyāhāra (withdrawal of the senses); Dhāraṇā (concentration); Dhyāna (meditation); and Samādhi (the final state of absorbed concentration and liberation). This system synthesised several earlier Indian philosophical traditions — Sāṃkhya metaphysics (the duality of Puruṣa/consciousness and Prakṛti/matter), Buddhist meditation psychology, and the existing yoga tradition — into the most systematic account of the path to liberation in Hindu thought.

The Yoga Sūtras were largely unknown in the West before the 19th century but became, through Swami Vivekananda's Raja Yoga (1896) and subsequent translations, the primary textual authority for the global yoga movement — the most widely practiced physical and spiritual discipline of the early 21st century.","subjects":["Sanskrit Literature","Ancient Era","Indian Philosophy","Yoga","Hindu Thought","Patanjali","Meditation","Liberation Philosophy"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Yoga Sutras of Patanjali have had a profound impact on the development of yoga and its global spread, shaping the practices and philosophies of millions of people around the world.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
